From: dsanke <dsanke1997@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: never bind host-destined frames into the PPE

The bridge-flow binding path (PPE L2 cache-miss fallback) resolves the
destination MAC through the bridge FDB and programs a hardware FOE
entry for it.  It relies only on the bridge FDB returning -EOPNOTSUPP
for the bridge's own MAC to keep host-bound management traffic in
software.  That implicit exclusion is fragile: if the router's MAC is
ever present in the FDB as a regular (non-local) entry, or the packet
is PACKET_HOST on the raw netdev, LuCI/SSH/SCP traffic to the router
gets bound into the PPE and black-holed.

Make the exclusion explicit: never prepare a hardware bridge subflow
for frames whose destination is the local host (PACKET_HOST, or the
bridge master's own MAC).  Such frames must always be punted to the
CPU.

Signed-off-by: dsanke <dsanke1997@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
@@ -1009,6 +1009,19 @@
 	if (!master)
 		return false;
 
+	/* Never bind frames destined to the local host (the router itself)
+	 * into the PPE: management traffic (LuCI/SSH/SCP) must always be
+	 * punted to the CPU.  The bridge FDB happens to report no forward
+	 * path for the bridge's own MAC, but make the exclusion explicit so
+	 * a stray FDB/PPE classification can never offload host-bound flows
+	 * into hardware and black-hole them.
+	 */
+	if (skb->pkt_type == PACKET_HOST ||
+	    ether_addr_equal(eh->h_dest, master->dev_addr)) {
+		dev_put(master);
+		return false;
+	}
+
 	rcu_read_lock();
 	ctx.dev = master;
 	if (is_vlan_dev(skb->dev)) {
